  "text": "To find the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere as a function of the coordinates of point \\(P(x,y,z)\\) on the surface of the cube, where the vertices of the cube lie on the axes, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the vertices of the cube:**\n   The vertices of the cube are \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\) due to the center of the cube being at the origin and the edge length being 2.\n\n2. **Equation of the sphere:**\n   The general equation of a sphere with center \\((h,k,l)\\) and radius \\(r\\) is:\n   \\[\n   (x-h)^2 + (y-k)^2 + (z-l)^2 = r^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the equation of the circumscribed sphere:**\n   \n   Since the sphere is circumscribed around the cube, it passes through all the vertices of the cube \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\). Hence, these points satisfy the equation of the sphere.\n\n   Therefore, substituting the vertices of the cube into the sphere's equation:\n   \\[\n   1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2 = r^2 \\implies 3 = r^2 \\implies r = \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Find the center of the sphere:**\n   Now, we know that the distance from the center \\((h,k,l)\\) to any of the vertices of the cube must be equal to the radius of the sphere \\(\\sqrt{3}\\). Hence:\n   \\[\n   h^2 + k^2 + l^2 = (\\sqrt{3})^2 = 3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   The center of the circumscribed sphere is \\((h,k,l)\\) where \\(h^2 + k^2 + l^2 = 3\\). Since the coordinate axes do not intersect with the centers of the cube, all coordinates of the center can be any point where their squares sum up to 3.\n\nSo, the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere are \\(\\boxed{(h,k,l)}\\) where \\(h^2 + k^2 + l^2 = 3\\).",
